WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:08.913
Osprey has a CPU profiler built into VS Code. Here's how to find your hot code, read your memory, and check a program for leaks.

00:09.663 --> 00:18.259
Open a program, and press Run and Profile CPU in the Processes view. Osprey builds it, starts it under the debugger, and starts sampling.

00:18.459 --> 00:22.974
The count climbs while it runs, and the panel names the hottest function so far.

00:23.174 --> 00:25.763
To stop, click the counter in the status bar.

00:26.513 --> 00:33.036
The results open on their own. The flame graph shows where the time went: the wider the frame, the hotter the code.

00:33.236 --> 00:38.849
Back in the source, each hot line carries its share of the samples, so collatz stands out at a glance.

00:45.113 --> 00:56.970
You can also profile a program that's already running, with no restart. Build it with dash dash profile so its samples carry source lines, start it from the terminal, and it shows up in Processes.

00:57.170 --> 01:02.116
Hover its row to see its CPU and memory, then press the flame to start sampling.

01:02.316 --> 01:04.646
Stop it the same way, from the status bar.

01:08.180 --> 01:14.431
Memory works differently in Osprey. Values never change once they're built, so the heap can never hold a cycle.

01:14.631 --> 01:23.530
You choose a memory backend when you build: the default, a tracing GC, or ARC, which frees each value the moment its last owner lets go.

01:23.730 --> 01:32.076
Osprey has no live heap tracker yet, so the profiler doesn't pretend to offer one. Each row in Processes shows the program's memory.

01:32.826 --> 01:38.112
To check for leaks, build with ARC, and run with OSPREY_ARC_DEBUG set to one.

01:38.312 --> 01:47.408
When the program exits, ARC counts every object still alive. Zero means nothing leaked. Set it to two, and it lists each survivor by kind.

01:50.926 --> 01:57.937
Both programs are in the Osprey repo, under examples slash profiling. Find more at ospreylang.dev.
